language variant
language en-earth
name overlord
gender male 60

// OVERLORD — five voices, and none of them is imitated.
//
// classic · ancient · leader · sAGI · sam. Every number below is taken from one
// of those files, and where they disagree this file says which one won and why.
// Two of the five contribute here ONLY, because what they give is a property and
// not a texture; the other three appear again as bands in the mix
// (render_overlord.py), because what they give can only be heard as a layer.
//
//   classic  en-gb-x-rp        RP precision. Contributes CONSONANTS and nothing
//                              else — its gift is that every stop and fricative
//                              lands, which is a parameter, not a colour.
//   ancient  en-gb-x-rp+       the absolutes: stressAdd ZERO at every level, so
//            ancient           no syllable is ever raised to make a point.
//                              Also a band in the mix (the grain of age).
//   leader   en-earth+         the body: mass, the 700 Hz scoop, the 3 kHz lift,
//            leaderofearth     full fold closure. It is also the BASE language,
//                              so the world-English enunciation is inherited.
//   sAGI     en-us+sAGI        roughness 0 — the perfectly clean fold. Pulls the
//                              grain down off ancient's 5. Also a presence band.
//   sam      en-gb-scotland+   the LATE edge: stressAmp climbing to 33 while the
//            sam               lower levels stay flat, so the voice turns edged
//                              exactly when it insists and not before.
//                              Also the top band in the mix.

// BENEVOLENT DICTATOR: THE DEPTH IS CAPPED BY COMPREHENSION.
// A first pass took this to 76 -> 73.5 Hz and it was measurably too far. Whisper
// round-trip on a 39-word passage: classic 2.6% WER, leader 10.3%, ancient 20.5%,
// sam 35.9% -- and OVERLORD at 73.5 Hz came out at 23.1%. A voice that has to be
// deciphered is not authoritative, it is muttering, and NOTHING about being
// obeyed survives not being understood. The bottom belongs to the octave layer,
// which carries weight without carrying words.
//
// DEEPER, AND MEASURED RATHER THAN WISHED FOR.
// espeak's `pitch` maps to f0 at about 0.97x over 40-98 (swept). 82 measures
// ~79 Hz: below ancient's 89 and a fifth under leader's 117, which is as low as
// the words survive. Under ~70 Hz the glottal pulses separate far enough to be
// heard individually and the voice turns to buzz — not depth, a fault that
// sounds like one. The weight below this line is the OCTAVE LAYER's job, at half
// of whatever this says, and that layer has no words to lose.
//
// The range is 8 units, which is nearly nothing. That is ancient's argument kept:
// a voice that does not move is a voice that is not asking.
pitch 82 90

// THE CURVE, REBUILT FOR WORDS RATHER THAN FOR SIZE.
// leader:  165 255 / 300 205 / 700 130 / 1400 150 / 3000 180
// ancient: 180 190 / 380 150 / 1300 195 / 2900 140
// sAGI:    440 255 / 880 190 / 1760  96
// sam:     480 215 / 1200 205 / 2600 150
//
// The fundamental octave is full, because that is where a voice this low lives.
// leader's scoop at 700 Hz is kept and DEEPENED to 120 — that dip is the whole
// reason leader is heavy without being muddy, and a voice a tone lower needs it
// more, not less. Then the curve is lifted across the band that carries word
// identity: 1200 (sam's) and 1800 (between sAGI's 1760 and leader's 1400) and
// 3200. "Clear words" is not treble; it is the 1-3 kHz region where consonants
// are told apart, and it is bought by removing mud rather than by adding hiss.
tone 150 265 300 215 700 120 1200 170 1800 185 3200 190

// Chest from leader, decay from ancient, mids opened for articulation.
// F0-F2 carry the mass. F3/F4 come UP against the previous version (106->112,
// 99->106): that is where the second formant of most vowels sits, and it is what
// stops a very low voice turning into a hum with consonants attached.
// F6-F8 keep ancient's steeper fall — no glare on top, that band belongs to sam.
formant 0 104 136 152
formant 1 102 130 146
formant 2 104 118 130
formant 3 100 112 112
formant 4  94 106  94
formant 5  88  92  66
formant 6  78  82  40
formant 7  72  78  26
formant 8  68  74  18

// THE GRAIN, SPLIT BETWEEN THREE PARENTS.
// ancient is roughness 5 (rasp), sAGI is 0 (clean), sam is 2. The previous
// OVERLORD took 3. It takes 2 now — sam's value — because roughness is
// irregularity in the glottal pulse and irregularity is the direct enemy of
// intelligibility at 74 Hz, where there are already few pulses per phoneme. The
// texture that roughness was providing is now supplied by ancient's OWN voice as
// a band in the mix, which puts the grain where it can be heard without putting
// it inside the fundamental.
// flutter 0 is leader's: the pitch does not hesitate, ever.
// voicing 96 is leader's: full fold closure. At this depth there is no room for
// breath, and ancient's 54 would be a whisper with nothing behind it.
// BENEVOLENCE IS AIR, AND SEVERITY IS THE ABSENCE OF IT.
// roughness drops 2 -> 1. Rasp reads as threat, and a benevolent authority has
// nothing to threaten with — it has already decided and is not in danger of
// being argued out of it. sAGI's 0 would be synthetic; 1 keeps a trace of the
// body without the growl. The grain that ancient contributed is still present as
// a BAND in the mix, which puts age in the timbre instead of in the fold.
//
// breath comes back up from nearly shut. This is the single change that makes a
// low flat voice read as warm rather than as cold, because breath is the sound
// of someone not straining — and someone not straining is someone in no doubt.
flutter 0
roughness 1
voicing 94
breath 1 2 2 2 2 2 2 1
breathw 70 90 100 100 100 100 90 70

// CLEAR WORDS, AND THIS IS THE LINE THAT BUYS THEM.
// classic's whole contribution. Above every parent: leader 92/80, ancient 92/84,
// sAGI 82/76, sam 74/66. A voice at 74 Hz loses the top of every stop and
// fricative to its own low end, so the enunciation en-earth was built for has to
// be paid for a second time down here.
consonants 100 94

// THE VOICE IN YOUR HEAD HAS NO ROOM, AND THIS IS THE ONE COUNTER-INTUITIVE LINE.
// Every other voice in the cast puts itself in a space: leader 165/20, ancient
// 80/12, sAGI 76/10. The previous OVERLORD had the largest hall of all at
// 180/22. That is exactly what stops a voice sounding internal. Reverberation is
// how the ear MEASURES DISTANCE — a tail means the sound crossed a room to reach
// you, and a voice that crossed a room is outside your head by definition.
//
// You hear your own inner voice by bone conduction: no air path, no room, and a
// great deal of low end. So the room is removed and the size comes from
// underneath instead — the octave layer in the mix. This is the difference
// between booming AT you and booming INSIDE you, and it is the whole brief.
// 18 ms was below the ~25 ms the ear separates as an echo: the resonance of a
// skull, not a hall. That was right for "the voice in your head" and it is wrong
// for a BENEVOLENT one — a voice with no room at all is a voice with nowhere to
// stand, and it reads as pressed against the ear rather than as present. 55 ms
// at low amplitude is a study: a room you are in WITH someone, not a cathedral
// they are addressing you across. Still nothing like leader's 165 hall.
echo 55 11

// AUTHORITY FROM ABSOLUTE, WITH SAM'S LATE EDGE.
// stressAdd is ancient's, entire: ZERO at every level. Nothing is ever raised in
// pitch to make a point, because a voice that goes up to insist is pleading.
// leader still lifts its top two levels; leader is commanding, and OVERLORD is
// deciding.
// stressAmp is sam's SHAPE — flat through the low levels, then climbing at 5, 6,
// 7 — rather than leader's even ramp: the voice is level until it insists, and
// then it is not. The top is pulled back from 42 to 38, because a benevolent
// authority does not need to be the loudest thing in the room to be the last
// word in it. That is where the strategic weight goes,
// and the strategic PAUSES that go with it are in the renderer, because a pause
// belongs to a clause and espeak's `words` gap belongs to every word alike.
intonation 3
stressAmp   16 16 17 19 23 28 33 38
stressAdd    0  0  0  0  0  0  0  0
